markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Task List (Lesson Overview)

<p>
In the upcoming activity from <strong>Lesson 3</strong>, students will build a simple <strong>Task List</strong> app while learning how JavaScript arrays can be updated as information changes.
</p>

<div style="display:flex; gap:24px; align-items:flex-start; flex-wrap:wrap; margin:24px 0;">

  <!-- Image -->
  <div style="flex:1; min-width:250px;">
    <img src="https://images.code.org/484b1d57a78c9c157a09aa50dcde634d-image-1784044143867.png"
         alt="Task List app activity preview"
         style="width:80%; border-radius:10px;">
  </div>

  <!-- Text -->
  <div style="flex:1; min-width:350px;">

<h2>Activity Sequence</h2>

  <p style="margin-top:0;">
  The activity is divided into a sequence of short levels. In each level, students use a different array method to modify the task list:
  </p>

  <ul style="margin-top:8px; line-height:1.7;">
    <li><code>push()</code> adds a new task to the end of the list.</li>
    <li><code>unshift()</code> adds a high-priority task to the beginning.</li>
    <li><code>shift()</code> removes the first task.</li>
    <li><code>pop()</code> removes the last task.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
  As students progress, they see how each method changes both the array and the app, reinforcing the connection between the code and the user interface.
  </p>

  </div>

</div>

<p>
In this professional learning module, you'll experience the activity from the student's perspective by completing <strong>only the first level</strong>, where you'll use <code>push()</code> to add a new task to the task list.
</p>

<p>
This focused practice lets you experience the instructional flow before exploring the rest of the sequence with students.
</p>


MARKDOWN
